<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Peel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1788">1788</a>–<a href="/years/1850">1850</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sir Robert Peel, 2nd Baronet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Robert Peel was a British Conservative statesman who served as Prime Minister of the United Kingdom in 1834–1835 and 1841–1846 and is regarded as the father of modern British policing. As Home Secretary he reformed the criminal law and founded the Metropolitan Police, whose officers became known as bobbies and peelers, and backed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-catholic-relief-act-1829">Roman Catholic Relief Act 1829</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamworth">Tamworth</a> Manifesto of 1834 laid down principles on which the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservative-party-uk">Conservative Party</a> is based. In his second ministry he cut tariffs and introduced a 3 per cent income tax, and after the outbreak of the Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-famine-ireland">Irish Famine</a> he joined with Whigs and Radicals to repeal the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corn-laws">Corn Laws</a>, which led to his resignation in 1846.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-peel.jpg" alt="Robert Peel" width="330" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Linnell · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Robert_Peel,_2nd_Bt_by_John_Linnell.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert Peel was born on 5 February 1788 at Chamber Hall in Bury, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a>, the son of Sir Robert Peel, 1st Baronet, one of the richest textile manufacturers of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a>, and of Ellen Yates. He took a double first in classics and mathematics at Christ Church, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, and entered Parliament in 1809, aged 21, for the Irish rotten borough of Cashel, where he was elected unopposed by 24 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a>. His sponsor was Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington">Arthur Wellesley</a>, the future Duke of Wellington. His maiden speech in 1810 was described by the Speaker, Charles Abbot, as the best first speech since that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-younger">William Pitt the Younger</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Peel became Under-Secretary of State for War and the Colonies in 1810 and Chief Secretary for Ireland in 1812. His Peace Preservation Act 1814 allowed paid special constables to be appointed in disturbed counties, later called peelers, and laid the basis for the Royal Irish Constabulary. He entered the cabinet as Home Secretary in 1822. He cut the number of crimes punishable by death, consolidated criminal statutes into what are called Peel's Acts, and in the Gaols Act 1823 introduced pay for gaolers and education for inmates.</p><p>In 1829 he established the Metropolitan Police for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> at Scotland Yard, with about 1,000 constables nicknamed bobbies or peelers. Unpopular at first, they cut crime, and by 1857 every British city had to form its own force. The nine Peelian Principles often credited to him were written by Charles Reith in 1948 as a summary of the force's 1829 Instructions. Peel, once a supporter of legal discrimination against Catholics, backed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-catholic-relief-act-1829">Roman Catholic Relief Act 1829</a>, judging civil strife a greater danger than emancipation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>William IV invited Peel to form a government in December 1834, when he was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, so <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> acted as caretaker for three weeks. His minority ministry issued the Tamworth Manifesto, pledging modest reform, and was defeated in the Commons on 7 April 1835 by 285 votes to 258 over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-ireland">Church of Ireland</a> revenues. It resigned having established the Ecclesiastical Commission.</p><p>He won a majority in the election of July 1841, in a recession with a budget deficit of £7.5 million. His second ministry passed the Mines and Collieries Act 1842, the Income Tax Act 1842, the Factories Act 1844 and the Railway Regulation Act 1844. Historians note that he was an intellectual convert to free trade from the 1820s. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corn-laws">Corn Laws</a> were repealed on 29 June 1846 and he resigned. Leading the Peelites, he stayed influential until his death on 2 July 1850, and was said by some critics to be a traitor to Toryism and by others, like Thomas Carlyle, a reforming Hercules.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Robert Peel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Reform Act 1832 widened the franchise and weakened royal and aristocratic control of seats, and by the mid-19th century, after <b>Robert Peel</b> had failed to govern without a majority in 1834–1835, it was settled that a government defeated in the Commons must resign or call an election.</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In Britain <b>Robert Peel</b>’s Tamworth Manifesto of 1834 pledged moderate reform and gave the party the name Conservative.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/conservatism">Conservatism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>In Britain Smith’s economics was put into practice with lower tariffs in the 1820s, and utilitarian reasoning, which classical liberals often used to justify inaction against poverty, also produced <b>Robert Peel</b>’s Metropolitan Police, the workhouses and asylums.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>Napoleon liberalized French economic policy, and the repeal of the Corn Laws under <b>Robert Peel</b> in 1846 symbolized the emergence of free trade; by the mid-19th century Britain and France had rejected mercantilism, and the British Empire used its power as the world’s financial centre to promote free…</blockquote><a href="/ideology/mercantilism">Mercantilism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Constables of <b>Robert Peel</b>'s new Metropolitan Police began patrolling London.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-29">29 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ramsbottom, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Westminster, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.65,-2.32&amp;year=1828" aria-label="Ramsbottom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4499 320 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Robert Peel"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4949 590h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4949 590h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.65,-2.32&amp;year=1828">What was here in 1828? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
